[
  "[SCENE: Cam on desk, SCNA 2011 photo queued]\nThis is The Sound Above. Episode 1.\nI'm Mike Hall. Today we're going back to November 19, 2011.\nOn-site at Software Craftsmanship North America. The Swissotel. Downtown Chicago.\n[FREEFORM: Talk about that building - the lobby, the elevator, the feeling of being at SCNA for the first time.\nWhat you were carrying. Who you knew. Who you didn't know yet.]\nMy first interview for UGtastic was with Sergio Pereira.\nOrganizer of Chicago Alt.NET.\n[PAUSE]\nThere's a reason Sergio was first.\nChicago Alt.NET wasn't the first user group I ever walked into.\nIt was the first user group where I ever stood up and gave a presentation.\n[SHOW: SCNA 2011 photo if available, or the Swissotel exterior]\n",
  "[SCENE: Slide or browser - show the Alt.NET Manifesto Wayback Machine link]\nTo understand why that room mattered, you have to remember what enterprise .NET felt like in 2007.\n[FREEFORM: Paint the picture - the WebForms world. ViewState. The page lifecycle. How you felt\nabout the default Microsoft toolchain before Alt.NET. What people actually said to you about NHibernate.]\nMost corporate shops treated MSDN as the law.\nIf a tool didn't ship inside Visual Studio, you weren't supposed to touch it.\nOn April 10, 2007, David Laribee published the Alt.NET Manifesto.\n[SHOW: web.archive.org/web/20070701125236/http://laribee.com/blog/2007/04/10/altnet/]\nFour tenets. Worth reading exactly as written:\nOne: You use what works while keeping an eye out for a better way.\nTwo: You reach outside the mainstream to adopt the best of any community.\nThree: You are not content with the status quo.\nFour: You know tools are great, but they only take you so far.\nThat October, developers gathered in Austin for the first Alt.NET Open Spaces.\nMartin Fowler documented it in his Bliki.\n[FREEFORM: Talk about Sergio specifically - when did you first encounter him, what was the energy\nof the 84th-floor Sears Tower meetings, what it meant to have regular people giving talks to peers\ninstead of polished vendor speakers]\nSecond Wednesday of every month. 6:30 PM. 84th floor of the Sears Tower.\nAbout 25 of us. Pizza we paid for ourselves.\nNovember 12, 2008: I gave my first presentation. NHaml. Adam Tybor on Spark.\n",
  "[SCENE: Cam]\nHere's the question guiding the rewatch today:\nHow do working engineers build practical autonomy inside corporate environments\nwithout waiting for vendor permission?\n[FREEFORM: Talk about the twatio.com story - the Twitter debate with Sergio about retweeting Hanselman.\nThe argument about whether retweets reach unique audiences. Building the ASP.NET MVC app to settle it empirically.\nTwatio later redirecting to ugtastic.com. That's the mindset in a nutshell: build a test.]\nWhen ASP.NET MVC was released it was a watershed.\nFor nearly a decade Redmond had insisted on WebForms, page lifecycles, viewstate.\nThe MVC pivot was the direct victory of the Ruby community.\nRails. Sinatra. Merb. Convention over configuration, lightweight routing, testable controllers.\nMicrosoft had to adapt because developers kept pointing at something better and saying: look.\nAlt.NET was never about hating .NET.\nAs Sergio says right at the start of this tape: we liked .NET, used it every day,\nand wanted continuous improvement, automated testing, NHibernate, StructureMap, IronRuby in our daily work.\n",
  "[SCENE: Switch to archive tape]\n[REWATCH: youtube.com/watch?v=qOHdZKz1WFw - cue from 00:00]\nLet's roll the 2011 hallway conversation with Sergio Pereira.\n[GO OFF CAMERA - react as you watch, don't over-script this section]\n[FREEFORM REACTION NOTES - things to watch for and react to:\n  - How Sergio describes the charter: not polished speakers, but people giving their lunch talks to a peer room\n  - The moment he talks about what people were actually arguing about (IoC, NHibernate)\n  - What the energy in that room felt like vs. a vendor conference\n  - What surprised you re-watching this now vs. how you remembered it\n  - Where Sergio's framing has aged well vs. where the landscape shifted completely]\n[SCENE: Back to cam after rewatch segment ends]\n[FREEFORM: Immediate reaction - what just stood out to you watching that back in 2026.\nWhat sounds prescient. What sounds like it was ahead of its time and still is.]\n",
  "[SCENE: Cam or terminal]\nHow does this connect to where we sit in 2026?\n[FREEFORM: Talk through the parallel directly - no need to script this one hard.\nThe through-line is: default vendor trap then was drag-and-drop wizards hiding coupling.\nDefault vendor trap now is black-box cloud AI generation hiding domain boundaries.\nThe lesson is identical: tools are instruments, not religions.\nYou judge every tool by whether it holds up under test.\nFast local verification loops. Clean domain models. You own the output.]\nIn 2008, the trap was designer wizards that made unit testing impossible.\nIn 2026, the trap is code generation that produces thousands of lines you can't verify.\nThe Chicago Alt.NET lesson is unchanged.\n[PAUSE]\n",
  "[SCENE: Outro card with links]\nThanks for joining Episode 1 of The Sound Above.\nFull interactive transcript with Sergio Pereira is on the Episode 1 page at just3ws.com.\nLinks there to David Laribee's 2007 Manifesto, Martin Fowler's Austin notes,\nScott Guthrie's early MVC previews, and Jeremy Miller's retrospectives.\n[FREEFORM: Personal close - something about what it means to you to have this tape out in the world,\nto have Sergio's voice preserved and searchable. Keep it short. Keep it honest.]\nTo support the independent compute and archive preservation:\npatreon.com/whoistechcmty\nIn Episode 2 we sit down with Ray Hightower of ChicagoRuby.\nGrassroots hospitality. Opening doors across communities.\nUntil next time: keep your feedback loops fast, and your code humane.\n"
]